            "text": "At the edge of town stood a library whose windows faced the sea. Every morning, Mara unlocked its blue door before sunrise and listened to the building wake: pipes sighed, floorboards answered, and somewhere in the attic a pigeon arranged its thoughts.\n\nShe believed libraries were not silent places but patient ones. They held every noise that had ever been pressed between pages: thunder in old voyages, laughter in letters, arguments in the margins of philosophy books. Her task was not merely to shelve them, but to make room for new weather.\n\nOne gray afternoon, a boy arrived carrying a broken umbrella and no library card. He asked whether there was a book about how to stop being afraid of storms.\n\nMara considered the question seriously. Then she led him to a narrow aisle where the ceiling leaked whenever rain grew bold. She chose a story about a lighthouse keeper who learned to name each kind of wind. The boy read beside the leak until the clouds broke open above the town. Rain rattled the glass like handfuls of bright nails. He flinched once, then twice, then smiled when he noticed the lighthouse keeper was frightened too.\n\nAt closing time, Mara found the umbrella standing open in the hallway. Its torn canopy caught drops from the ceiling, turning them into a small, shining storm. On the desk lay a note: \u201cI think storms are just the sky reading aloud.\u201d Mara placed it in the returned books, where it waited quietly."
